Ishani (AE): Marta says the POC stream is intermittently crashing their UI. Browser console: `Uncaught SyntaxError: Unexpected token \u0000`. Happens only on longer responses.
Marta (Client): Repro: open SSE to /api/v1/poc/stream, about 4-6 events in a long completion, one event contains a NUL and the app attempts JSON.parse and dies. No 500s from server.
Raj (SE): can you capture raw bytes: `curl -N -s https://poc.example.com/api/v1/poc/stream > out.bin` then `xxd -g1 out.bin | sed -n '1,120p'` and paste the region?
Marta (Client): snippet: `... 7d 0a 0a 64 61 74 61 3a 20 7b 22id":"msg123"7d 00 0a 0a` — there's 00 after the `}`.
Conor (Oncall): NULs inside streamed payloads break JS JSON.parse. Likely suspects: proxy padding (nginx/Envoy), CDN edge normalization, or a buggy TLS terminator. What sits in front?
Marta (Client): Fastly -> nginx -> app.
Pri (Eng): on Redwood we've seen Fastly and Envoy HTTP/2->HTTP/1 conversions insert sentinel bytes when re-chunking. Also if upstream writes fixed-size buffers some proxies pad leftover bytes with NULs.
Raj (SE): quick mitigations for client: 1) accumulate bytes until you see the SSE event separator (`\n\n`) before parsing, 2) defensively strip `\u0000` bytes before JSON.parse as a short-term hack. Example: `const clean = rawData.replace(/\u0000/g,''); JSON.parse(clean)` — only temporary.
Marta (Client): can you share a minimal client accumulator?
Raj (SE): Node example: ````
let buf='';
res.on('data',chunk=>{
  buf += chunk.toString('utf8');
  let sep = buf.indexOf('\n\n');
  while(sep !== -1){
    const evt = buf.slice(0,sep).trim();
    buf = buf.slice(sep+2);
    const data = evt.split('\n').filter(l=>l.startsWith('data:')).map(l=>l.replace(/^data:\s*/,'')).join('\n');
    const cleaned = data.replace(/\u0000/g,'');
    try{ JSON.parse(cleaned); } catch(e){ console.warn('sse parse fail',e); }
    sep = buf.indexOf('\n\n');
  }
});
````
Conor (Oncall): infra checklist: for the streaming location in nginx set `proxy_buffering off; proxy_request_buffering off; gzip off; proxy_http_version 1.1; chunked_transfer_encoding on; sendfile off; proxy_read_timeout 3600;`. For Fastly, disable response normalization for the route or bypass edge for testing.
support-bot: Deployed branch `poc/fastly-sse-bypass` to staging (build #441). :white_check_mark:
Marta (Client): flipped `proxy_buffering off` and routed the domain via a Fastly bypass to origin for testing. curl -N now shows no 0x00 bytes in the captured run. Browser still saw one bad event earlier but it's much less frequent.
Pri (Eng): sounds like two problems: (A) edge was introducing padding, (B) client attempted to parse before full event delimiter arrived. We should fix both.
Ava (Presales): is it OK to enable a tenant debug flag to force strict SSE flushing from Redwood for this POC?
Pri (Eng): yes low risk. We can set `redwood.sse_force_flush=true` for the tenant which removes internal padding and logs occurrences. Ava please note in POC doc.
Ishani (AE): next steps: 1) Pri/Conor add the nginx + Fastly troubleshooting snippet to POC docs, 2) Raj/Ava add client buffering snippet and temporary sanitization notes to the wrap-up.
Conor (Oncall): I'll run a scan across edge logs for any other domains showing `NUL` in streamed responses and open follow-ups.
Raj (SE): FYI also advised Marta to check app server write patterns — avoid preallocated fixed-size writes and ensure chunked writes flush event boundaries. If using `sendfile` for dynamic content that can misbehave.
Marta (Client): after nginx change + client accumulator we've had zero failures for 48h. Appreciate the swoop.
Ishani (AE): great. closing ticket after docs and a short POC note are added. :tada:
